  "answer": "I've completed the incremental deep-research review. Here's the summary:\n\n**Verified and buildable (ranked, not generic-survey):**\n\n- **Lane 1 (generative DMX)**: Skip-BART (arXiv:2506.01482) and SeqLight (arXiv:2605.03660) are both real, code+weights+data available (RS2002 GitHub/HF, RPMC-L2 dataset ~40GB). Neither was actually tested — your \"learned group decoder\" and \"per-frame regression\" experiments are architecturally distinct from Skip-BART's autoregressive skip-connection generator and SeqLight's two-stage decomposition policy. LightingGen (IEEE 2026) is real but I could not verify code/data availability — flagged as unconfirmed, do not build on it yet. LightMuse/AuraLuxMuse has no traceable primary paper — downgraded to a negative finding, not a build target.\n\n- **Lane 2 (form-aware generation)**: Whole-Song Hierarchical Diffusion (ICLR 2024) and SongFormer (arXiv:2510.02797, fully open-sourced) give you genuine chorus-reuse and pretrained structure-segmentation mechanisms distinct from your refuted \"structure-as-scalar-feature\" experiment. The ISMIR 2023 repetition-grammar paper (formal prototypes, CFG+ILP) is a completely untried mechanism class.\n\n- **Lane 3 (few-query preference)**: Sequential Line Search (Koyama, SIGGRAPH 2017, public C++/Python lib), Preferential Bayesian Optimization (Gonzalez et al. 2017), and Coactive Learning (Jain et al., IJRR 2015) are all real, code-available alternatives to your flawed passive-pairwise pilot — directly addressing your own postmortem that \"selection efficiency matters more than passively collecting pairs.\"\n\n- **Lane 4 (groove vs. sync)**: Witek et al. (inverted-U syncopation/pleasure) and the groove-components paper give you retrospective, zero-cost reanalysis of your existing 39-pair pilot data using metrics that actually match your qualitative feedback (\"waves > all-on-beat\").\n\nThe report also states explicitly what your Stage 9/10 failures do and don't rule out, and closes with 6 ranked experiments — 4 runnable on CPU right now (reusing existing pilot data, no new rendering), 2 requiring the 12GB GPU once free.",
